      "body": "> The description says all 150 `txhash` values are unchanged, but 60 of them change. They are the vectors whose selector gets padded (`8000` becomes `80000000`) and has TXFS_CONTROL (0x80) set, since that bit commits the selector bytes to the hash. The code is consistent: `cargo run` regenerates this PR's file byte for byte.\r\n> \r\n> The PR also rejects 2- and 3-byte selectors, which were accepted until now. That changes which selectors are valid, so I think the BIP authors should confirm it.\r\n\r\nThanks for catching this. I compared the vector files and confirmed that 60 of the 150 txhash values change; the other 90 do not. The changed hashes are from padded selectors with TXFS_CONTROL set, which commits the selector bytes. My PR description was incorrect, and I’ll update both the hash count and the cargo run wording.\r\nI also agree that rejecting 2- and 3-byte long-form selectors changes the accepted inputs of the reference implementation. I read “For both inputs and then outputs, expect an additional byte” as requiring both bytes, but this interpretation should be confirmed by the BIP authors before we treat the change as settled. Could the authors confirm the intended validity rule?",
